          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My home blood pressure has been around 150/95 for two weeks. Is that something I need to act on now, and what do the guidelines say the target should be?",
          "answer": "Your blood pressure reading of 150/95 mm Hg indicates stage 2 hypertension as per United States guidelines. While this does not require immediate action, it is important to take this seriously and consider discussing it with your clinician.\n\nGuidelines recommend that most adults aim for a target blood pressure below 130/80 mm Hg once treatment has begun (source: \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\"). Under the classification, normal blood pressure is below 120/80 mm Hg, and stage 1 hypertension is 130 to 139/80 to 89 mm Hg. Your reading is above these thresholds, falling into the stage 2 hypertensive category, which emphasizes the need for follow-up and management.\n\nIt is advisable to assess your overall health with a healthcare provider regarding your blood pressure readings. They may want to discuss lifestyle changes and possibly consider medications to help manage your blood pressure effectively.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
